# Why the Tsar fell, and why Lenin won
subtitle: "Why Russia's Tsar collapsed in a week in 1917, and how a small, organised party took power eight months later by promising peace, land and bread."
thumb: "band \"Lenin missed it\" stamp \"PLOT TWIST\" card wood"

## Why the Tsar fell, and why Lenin won
sources:
    - id: figes
      title: 'A People''s Tragedy: The Russian Revolution 1891–1924'
      authors: Orlando Figes
      year: 1996
      finding: the long and short causes of 1917; the February collapse and the Bolshevik rise
    - id: fitz
      title: The Russian Revolution (4th ed.)
      authors: Sheila Fitzpatrick
      year: 2017
      finding: dual power, the war and the land question; Bolshevik growth in 1917
    - id: brit
      title: Russian Revolution of 1917
      authors: Encyclopaedia Britannica
      finding: dates of February and October (Julian and Gregorian), the abdication, the Kornilov affair
    - id: losses
      title: War Losses (Russian Empire)
      authors: 1914-1918-online, International Encyclopedia of the First World War
      finding: by the end of 1916 Russia had lost about five million men killed, wounded or captured
    - id: ca
      title: 'Russia Goes to the Polls: The Election to the All-Russian Constituent Assembly, 1917'
      authors: Oliver H. Radkey
      year: 1989
      finding: Bolsheviks about 24 % of the vote; Socialist Revolutionaries about 40 %

