    card title "Why Russia had two revolutions"

B: Russia, 1917. In one year it loses its Tsar, and ends up ruled by Lenin. How?
    draw axis ticks_long

A: That's two puzzles, really: why the Tsar fell, and why the Bolsheviks, of all people, won.
    draw efeb

B: Isn't it one story? Lenin's revolutionaries overthrew the Tsar.
    point at.ref txt_efeb gesture circle

A: That's the usual picture, and it's wrong. When t (@draw swiss l_swiss@)he Tsar fell, Lenin was in Switzerland, reading about it in the papers.

    erase swiss l_swiss

A: Start with the long fuse. In 1861 the serfs were freed, but they had to pay for their land over forty-nine years.
    draw e1861 c_land

A: From the 1890s Russia industrialised fast. Peasants crowded into St Petersburg's factories, poorly paid and close to the throne.
    draw c_work

A: Over it all sat one man with absolute power: Nicholas the Second, Tsar from 1894, sure that God had chosen him.
    draw era l_era c_auto

A: In January 1905 troops fired on a peaceful march to his palace. Bloody Sunday.
    draw e1905 c_1905

A: A year of strikes and mutinies followed, and Nicholas gave way just enough: an elected parliament, the Duma.
    draw e1906 l_fuel

B: So Russia became a constitutional monarchy?
    point at.ref dot_e1906 gesture tap

A: On paper. He dissolved the first two Dumas within months, and kept the final word.
    highlight c_auto

B: Fine, that's the fuel. What lit it?
    draw l_spark

A: The war. By the end of 1916, around five million Russian soldiers had been killed, wounded or captured, and Nicholas had taken command himself.
    draw e1914 c_war a_auto_war

A: Prices had roughly quadrupled, the railways carried soldiers before grain, and the capital ran short of bread and fuel.
    draw c_bread

A: On the twenty-third of February 1917, Women's Day, women textile workers walked out. Within days the city was on strike.
    draw c_strike

B: Wait. February? I always heard March.
    point at.ref txt_efeb gesture underline

A: Both are right. Russia still kept the old calendar, thirteen days behind the West's.
    draw cal

    erase cal a_auto_war

    fade e1861 e1905 e1906 e1914 ticks_long l_era era to 0.3

A: Then the turning point: on the twenty-seventh, the soldiers sent to put the crowds down joined them instead.
    draw c_mutiny

    highlight c_mutiny effect pulse

A: His generals told Nicholas the army would not back him, and on the second of March he abdicated.
    draw a_fuel a_spark c_fall

B: So nobody planned it. Everything gave way at once.
    flow a_fuel a_spark

    erase causes a_fuel a_spark era l_era ticks_long e1861 e1905 e1906 e1914

    move efeb by [-650.6,0] duration 2.5

    draw ticks_1917 parallel true

A: Right, and the real puzzle is the next eight months. Power was now split.  (@draw d_gov d_sov@)A Provisional Government of liberals, and the Petrograd Soviet, a council of workers' and soldiers' delegates.

B: And whom did the soldiers obey?
    point at.ref d_sov gesture circle

A: The Soviet. Its Order Number One said: obey the government only when the Soviet agrees. Lenin called it dual power.
    draw d_arrow l_veto l_dual

    erase dual d_arrow l_veto l_dual

A: Then the government made its fateful choice: stay in the war, and leave the land to a future assembly.
    draw col_rows col_mod

B: Why on earth stay in?
    highlight g_m1

A: Loyalty to the allies, fear of Germany, and loans from Britain and France. The trenches didn't care.
    point at.ref g_m1 gesture circle

A: In April, Lenin arrived at the Finland Station. Germany had let him cross by train, hoping he'd take Russia out of the war.
    draw elenin n_train

    hide promise

    scale members cites ["fitz"] x 120 y 270 width 760 label "Bolshevik members, Feb 1917" unit members min 1000 max 1000000 value 24000 log true tick_format words markers [{"value":240000,"label":"by autumn","color":"#b5482e"}]

    ask "How many members did Lenin's party have in February 1917? Click on the line." on members store m default "100000" relative true
        right: Close: about twenty-four thousand, in an empire of some 170 million.
        wrong: You said {m}; it was about twenty-four thousand, in an empire of some 170 million.

A: By autumn they had roughly ten times as many.
    draw members_marker_1

    erase members

    show promise

A: They grew because Lenin said what everyone wanted to hear: all power to the Soviets, and peace, land, bread.
    draw col_bol

A: In July, after a failed offensive, armed soldiers and sailors filled the streets. The revolt was crushed, and Lenin fled to Finland.
    draw ejuly n_fin

B: So that's the end of Lenin, then?
    point at.ref n_fin gesture underline

A: Not quite. In late August the army's chief, General Kornilov, sent troops toward the capital.
    draw ekorn

A: Kerensky, now heading the government, armed the workers and let jailed Bolsheviks out to stop him. Kornilov's march fell apart.
    draw n_turn x_korn

A: By September they led the Petrograd Soviet. On the twenty-fifth of October, our seventh of November, their Red Guards took the capital and, overnight, the Winter Palace.
    draw eoct n_palace

    hide promise

B: Surely by then most Russians were behind them.
    scale vote cites ["ca"] x 120 y 270 width 760 label "Bolshevik vote, Nov 1917" unit % min 0 max 100 value 24

    ask "In November's free election, what share of the vote went to the Bolsheviks? Click on the line." on vote store v default "50"
        right: About a quarter. A peasant party, the Socialist Revolutionaries, won far more, and in January the Bolsheviks shut the assembly down.
        wrong: You said {v}; it was about a quarter. A peasant party, the Socialist Revolutionaries, won far more, and in January the Bolsheviks shut the assembly down.

    erase vote elenin ejuly ekorn x_korn n_train n_fin n_turn ticks_1917

    show promise

B: So the Tsar fell because everything gave way at once. But Lenin won because…
    highlight txt_efeb txt_eoct

A: …his party was organised, and it promised peace and land now, while the moderates kept saying later.
    draw box_bol

    quiz "Suppose in May 1917 the government had made peace and let the peasants take the land. What happens to the Bolsheviks' chances?"
        right: They lose their best argument: peace and land now was what drew soldiers and peasants to Lenin, and the moderates had refused it.
        wrong: Think about what drew soldiers and peasants to the Bolsheviks, and who had refused to give it.
        + They lose their best argument
        * They rise: peace frees Lenin's hands
        * No change: they had German money

